rmalmap, rendered using the normalmap shader that when rendered has some obvious seams in the normals near the UV borders, see http://hnsr.nl/files/prog/3js-normal-test/index2.html (Tested with Chrome 26.0.1410.19 beta and Firefox 19, on Linux with an NVIDIA GTX275 using NVIDIA driver version 313.18).\n\nIn issue #1647, someone seems to have the same problem (not the tesselation issue though, my model only has triangles), and provided some code that splits vertices along the UV seams which fixes the issue for me, see http://hnsr.nl/files/prog/3js-normal-test/index.html\n\nI'm not exactly sure why that fix works, because from what I understand you can only have one set of indices in OpenGL that index the combined vertices properties (position, normal, other attributes) rather than being able to have them in separate arrays and indexing them seperately with multiple sets of indices, so I tried to look for a possible bug where the arrays are merged into one, but I couldn't actually find any such place in the three.js code (in fact it seems the arrays are uploaded directly to OpenGL, so maybe I'm wrong about all of this).\n\nIn any case, I'm a bit confused, but the model also renders correctly using some old C/OpenGL code or when I reimport it in blender so I figured I'd file an issue for it anyway, even though the code from the previously mentioned issue fixes it\n","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mrdoob/three.js/issues/3169/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/mrdoob/three.js/issues/3169/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":"completed"}},"event":"cross-referenced"}]